quadrf-mesh for Debian
----------------------

Both systemd units are installed disabled by default. You can start them from
the QuadRF Applications launcher, or with:

  sudo systemctl start quadrf-lora-phy quadrf-meshtasticd

`quadrf-lora-phy` starts in OTA mode (`QUADRF_LORA_PHY_MODE=--ota` in `/etc/default/quadrf-lora-phy`).
Set `QUADRF_LORA_PHY_MODE=--digital` for FPGA digital loopback.

Starting the mesh automatically opens the QuadRF Mesh Monitor desktop window
and makes the Meshtastic web UI available at:

  https://<QUADRF_HOSTNAME>.local:9443/

The monitor lists every CRC-passing PHY decode, including this unit's own
transmissions (PHY journal ``echo-drop``). Those frames are not delivered
to Meshtastic.

That HTTPS port is the web UI only. Phone apps and the Meshtastic CLI use
PhoneAPI on TCP 4403, not 9443.

Radio configuration
-------------------

`quadrf-lora-phy` is authoritative for radio hardware settings. Frequency
(`QUADRF_LORA_PHY_FREQ`) and preset (`QUADRF_LORA_PHY_PRESET`) in
`/etc/default/quadrf-lora-phy` program the RF frontend at start. The
appliance GUI LO control can retune frequency until PHY restarts.

Meshtastic `lora.override_frequency` is kept at 0 and does not tune the
radio. `lora.modem_preset` is sent to PHY (Short Turbo or Short Fast):

  Preset mapping:
    shortturbo     SHORT_TURBO (default)
    shortfast      SHORT_FAST

  meshtastic --host 127.0.0.1:4403 --set lora.modem_preset SHORT_TURBO

Node identity
-------------

On first launch, the daemon derives a node MAC from the QuadRF hardware serial
and stores it in `/var/lib/meshtasticd/identity.env`. If you clone an OS image to
another board, it detects the serial mismatch and generates a fresh identity for
the new hardware. You can also specify an explicit MAC by setting
`QUADRF_MESHTASTICD_MAC` in `/etc/default/quadrf-meshtasticd`.
